    "title": "Trump Says Housing Prices Aren't Going Down Just So 'Someone Who Didn't Work Very Hard' Can Buy A Home— Going To Keep Homeowners 'Wealthy'",
    "text": "Benzinga and Yahoo Finance LLC may earn commission or revenue on some items through the links below. The housing market debate isn't really about drywall and shingles. It's about who gets rewarded — and who doesn't. \"People that own their homes, we’re gonna keep them wealthy. We’re gonna keep those prices up,\" said President Donald Trump during a January Cabinet meeting. \"We’re not gonna destroy the value of their homes so that somebody who didn’t work very hard can buy a home.\" That last line is the flashpoint. By framing it as protecting people who \"worked very hard\" to buy their homes, Trump positioned rising home values as a payoff for discipline — saving for a down payment, qualifying for a mortgage, riding out market swings. In that telling, equity isn't luck. It's earned. Don't Miss: The AI Marketing Platform Backed by Insiders from Google, Meta, and Amazon — Invest at $0.85/Share Own a Stake in California's New Standard for Luxury Behavioral Health The flip side of that framing is just as clear. Lowering home prices significantly could feel, to existing owners, like erasing years of effort. If someone bought at $350,000 and values slide 10%, that's $35,000 in paper equity gone. For households whose net worth is heavily tied to their home, that's not abstract. At the same time, affordability has become a real constraint. As of January, the median sales price for existing homes was $396,800. In 1996, the median existing home price was about $118,100. Even accounting for wage growth over time, prices have far outpaced incomes in many markets. National home prices rose just 1.3% in 2025, according to the S&P Cotality Case-Shiller Index. This was the slowest annual increase since 2011. Zillow projects roughly 0.9% growth through the end of 2026. That suggests stabilization — not a crash that would dramatically reset affordability. So what did he mean by \"someone who didn’t work very hard\"? Trending: Steve Jobs once worked at Atari. Now the brand that helped launch a tech icon is building a destination investors can help bring to life. In practical terms, it's a political way of saying he does not want policy to intentionally push prices down in order to make homes cheaper. A sharp drop might help new buyers in the short run, but it would also reduce the wealth of current owners. Many of those owners have spent years paying mortgages, property taxes and maintenance costs. The argument is that they should not see their asset devalued to create entry points for others.",
